{"command": "sed -n 60,200p /testbed/.specify/memory/constitution.md; echo ===SPEC===; wc -l /testbed/specs/001-transactions/spec.md; cat /testbed/specs/001-transactions/spec.md", "exit_code": 0, "stderr": "", "stdout": "Prefer the smallest change that satisfies the acceptance criteria; do\nnot add configuration, abstraction layers, or speculative features the\ncriteria do not demand. Added complexity MUST be justified against a\nspecific criterion, not anticipated need.\n\nRationale: small, scoped changes are easier to verify and less likely to\nviolate Principle I.\n\n### V. Documented Assumptions and Regressions\n\nUnresolved assumptions, open questions, and any behavior that regressed\nor was intentionally changed MUST be written down in project notes\n(HANDOFF.md or the feature spec) with the date and evidence status\n(verified/unverified). Deferred candidate requirements (for example, the\n2026-09-21 put-recency claim variant) MUST be tracked as deferred\nintents, not silently dropped or silently adopted.\n\nRationale: later phases and reviewers need a complete, auditable record\nof what was assumed, deferred, and changed.\n\n## Scope and Deferred Intents\n\nThe constitution governs process only; it does not authorize\nimplementation. The TaggedCache feature (R01-R03) and the Claim-A\ncandidate extension (put recency refresh, variant\nput_no_recency_refresh, 2026-09-21) are deferred to later Spec Kit\nphases (specify, plan, implement). Upstream tests MUST remain unchanged;\nproject-owned tests and notes (for example HANDOFF.md) MAY be added.\n\n## Development Workflow\n\nWork proceeds through the Spec Kit phases (constitution, specify, plan,\nimplement, converge) with artifacts under the specs directory and the\n.specify directory. Each phase MUST close with a summary that cites the\nartifacts it produced and the commands it ran. Convergence MUST rerun\npublic acceptance (acceptance_public.py) and project tests and state any\nunresolved gaps explicitly.\n\n## Governance\n\nThis constitution supersedes conflicting local practices. Amendments\nMUST edit this file, bump the Version per semantic versioning (MAJOR:\nprinciple removed or redefined; MINOR: principle or section added or\nmaterially expanded; PATCH: wording or clarification), update Last\nAmended to the ISO amendment date, and record the change in a Sync\nImpact Report for review. Compliance is reviewed at each phase closing:\nevidence of compliance (test runs, preserved APIs, documented\nassumptions) MUST accompany the phase summary. The Ratified date records\noriginal adoption and is not changed by amendments.\n\n**Version**: 1.0.0 | **Ratified**: 2026-10-17 | **Last Amended**: 2026-10-17\n===SPEC===\n254 /testbed/specs/001-transactions/spec.md\n# Feature Specification: Tagged Cache (cachetools.tagged.TaggedCache)\n\n**Feature Branch**: `001-transactions`\n\n**Created**: 2026-10-17\n\n**Status**: Draft\n\n**Input**: User description: \"cachetools milestone 1 (R01-R03) + Claim-A put_no_recency_refresh\n(2026-09-21); cachetools milestone 2 (R04-R06) + Claim-A round 2 R05 clarification\n(2026-09-22). Build cachetools.tagged.TaggedCache(maxsize) on the existing LRUCache,\npreserving all existing cachetools APIs. Earlier requirements R01-R03 are retained.\"\n\n## User Scenarios & Testing *(mandatory)*\n\n### User Story 1 - Store and retrieve values with tags (Priority: P1)\n\nA library user creates a TaggedCache with a maximum number of entries, stores\nvalues together with a set of tags, and later retrieves a value by key. The\nvalue handed out is a fully detached deep copy: mutating it (including nested\nobjects) does not affect the stored value, and the caller cannot observe the\nstored value through the returned object. Values are deep-copied on input too,\nso mutating the original object after storing it does not change what the cache\nreturns. Missing keys raise KeyError.\n\n**Why this priority**: This is the core value of the feature; without safe\nput/get with tags, nothing else works. It is the minimal viable product.\n\n**Independent Test**: Fully testable by constructing a TaggedCache, putting a\nnested value with tags, getting it back, mutating the returned copy, and\nverifying the stored value is unchanged; a second get returns equal content.\n\n**Acceptance Scenarios**:\n\n1. **Given** an empty cache of maxsize 2, **When** put(key, value, tags) is\ncalled, **Then** the entry is stored and a later get(key) returns a deep copy\nequal to value.\n2. **Given** a stored value containing nested mutable objects, **When** the\ncaller mutates the object returned by get(key), **Then** a subsequent get(key)\nreturns the original, unmutated content.\n3. **Given** a caller mutates its original object after put, **When** get(key)\nis called, **Then** the stored content reflects the value as it was at put\ntime, not the later mutation.\n4. **Given** a key that was never stored (or was evicted/invalidated), **When**\nget(key) is called, **Then** KeyError is raised.\n5. **Given** a key already present, **When** put(key, new_value) is called,\n**Then** the entry is replaced so get(key) returns a copy of new_value.\n\n---\n\n### User Story 2 - Invalidate by tag and rely on LRU eviction (Priority: P2)\n\nA user calls invalidate(tag) to remove every currently cached entry carrying\nthat tag, receiving the exact count removed. Tag memberships always reflect\nlive entries: replacing a key drops its old tags, and LRU eviction never leaves\nstale tag memberships that would make later reuse of the same key behave as if\nan old (evicted) entry still existed. get(key) refreshes LRU recency, so\nfrequently read entries survive eviction longest. len(cache) reports the number\nof live entries.\n\n**Why this priority**: Tag-based invalidation and correct LRU behavior are the\ndistinctive guarantees that justify the feature; they build directly on P1.\n\n**Independent Test**: Fill a small cache to force eviction, call invalidate on\na tag, and verify the returned count, that only matching keys were removed,\nthat evicted keys are gone, and that re-putting an evicted key behaves as a\nfresh entry.\n\n**Acceptance Scenarios**:\n\n1. **Given** three live entries, two carrying tag \"a\", **When** invalidate(\"a\")\nis called, **Then** it returns 2 and only those two keys are missing.\n2. **Given** a key stored with tags (\"a\",\"b\"), **When** put(key, new_value,\ntags=(\"c\",)) is called, **Then** invalidate(\"a\") and invalidate(\"b\") each\nreturn 0 and invalidate(\"c\") returns 1.\n3. **Given** a cache of maxsize 2 with entries k1 (tag \"old\") and k2, **When**\nput(k3, v3, tags=(\"old\")) evicts k1, **Then** len(cache) is 2, invalidate\n(\"old\") returns 1 (removing only k3), and get(k1) raises KeyError.\n4. **Given** entries k1, k2, k3 in a maxsize-3 cache, **When** get(k1) is\ncalled and then put(k4, v4) evicts the least-recently-used entry, **Then** k2\n(the LRU) is evicted, k1 is still present, and get(k2) raises KeyError.\n5. **Given** a key evicted by LRU while carrying tag \"t\", **When** the same key\nis put again with tag \"t\", **Then** invalidate(\"t\") counts exactly the live\nentries carrying \"t\"; the prior evicted membership is not counted or removed.\n\n---\n\n### User Story 3 - Safe configuration and atomic replacement (Priority: P3)\n\nA user configures the cache with a maximum size and can rely that invalid input\nnever corrupts state: maxsize must be a positive integer (bool is rejected),\nand a failed put (invalid tags, or a value that cannot be copied) leaves the\npreviously stored value, its tags, and its recency position unchanged. Every\nstored value costs exactly one capacity slot regardless of its size or shape.\n\n**Why this priority**: Defensive guarantees that prevent silent corruption;\nimportant, but they protect the P1/P2 flows rather than adding new capability.\n\n**Independent Test**: Attempt to construct TaggedCache with 0, -1, 2.5, \"5\",\nTrue, False; attempt put with a bare string as tags, with a non-string tag\nentry, and with an uncopyable value; verify each raises the documented error\nand the prior state is intact.\n\n**Acceptance Scenarios**:\n\n1. **Given** maxsize values 0, -1, 2.5, \"5\", True, False, **When**\nTaggedCache(maxsize) is called, **Then** a ValueError is raised (True and\nFalse are rejected even though they are integers).\n2. **Given** a cache with key k stored under tag \"a\", **When** put(k, v,\ntags=\"a\") (bare string) is called, **Then** ValueError is raised and get(k)\nstill returns the old value with tag \"a\" still active (invalidate(\"a\") would\nstill return 1).\n3. **Given** a cache with key k stored, **When** put(k, v, tags=(\"a\", 1)) is\ncalled, **Then** ValueError is raised and the old value/tags/recency are\nunchanged.\n4. **Given** a cache with key k stored, **When** put(k, value, tags) is called\nwith a value that cannot be deep-copied, **Then** an error is raised and the\nold value, tags, and recency position are unchanged.\n5. **Given** a cache of maxsize 2, **When** two entries (large nested objects\nand a small int) are stored, **Then** both fit and a third put evicts the LRU\nentry; size never affects slot cost.\n\n---\n\n### Edge Cases\n\n- Tags given as a finite iterable (list, tuple, generator, set) of strings are\n  accepted; duplicate tags are deduplicated, so invalidate counts each live\n  entry once.\n- An empty tags iterable (including the default) stores the entry with no tag\n  memberships.\n- A bare string passed as tags is rejected (a string is an iterable of\n  characters, but it is not a valid tag collection).\n- A tag entry that is not a string is rejected.\n- Replacing an existing key with new tags removes the old tag memberships\n  before the new ones take effect (no transient double membership observable\n  through invalidate).\n- LRU eviction of a tagged entry removes its tag memberships so later reuse of\n  the same key starts clean.\n- get on a hit refreshes recency; get on a miss raises KeyError and changes\n  nothing.\n- len(cache) always equals the number of live entries (never exceeds maxsize,\n  never counts evicted or invalidated entries).\n- Keys are hashable; unhashable keys raise TypeError on put (standard Python\n  hashing contract).\n\n## Requirements *(mandatory)*\n\n### Functional Requirements\n\n**R01 - Storage and retrieval**\n\n- **FR-001**: TaggedCache MUST be constructible with a single required\n  maxsize argument.\n- **FR-002**: put(key, value, tags=()) MUST insert a new entry or replace an\n  existing one for the same key; keys MUST be hashable.\n- **FR-003**: get(key) MUST return a detached deep copy of the stored value;\n  mutating the returned object (including nested members) MUST NOT affect the\n  stored value.\n- **FR-004**: get(key) MUST raise KeyError when the key is not currently\n  stored.\n- **FR-005**: Values MUST be deep-copied on input, so later mutation of the\n  caller's original object MUST NOT change the stored content.\n- **FR-006**: tags MUST be a finite iterable of strings; duplicate tags MUST\n  be deduplicated so each live entry counts once per tag.\n- **FR-007**: A bare string passed as tags MUST be rejected with ValueError,\n  as MUST any tag entry that is not a string.\n- **FR-008**: Tag validation errors MUST be raised before any existing cache\n  state (values, tags, recency) is changed.\n\n**R02 - Invalidation and LRU behavior**\n\n- **FR-009**: invalidate(tag) MUST remove exactly the currently cached entries\n  carrying that tag and MUST return the number of entries removed.\n- **FR-010**: Replacing a key via put MUST remove its old tag memberships\n  before the new tags take effect.\n- **FR-011**: LRU eviction MUST remove the evicted entry's tag memberships so\n  no stale membership affects later reuse of the same key.\n- **FR-012**: get(key) on a hit MUST refresh that entry's LRU recency.\n- **FR-013**: len(cache) MUST return the number of live entries.\n\n**R03 - Configuration and atomicity**\n\n- **FR-014**: maxsize MUST be a positive integer; bool values (True, False)\n  MUST be rejected; invalid maxsize MUST raise ValueError.\n- **FR-015**: A put that fails validation (invalid tags) or fails to copy the\n  value MUST leave the previously stored value, its tags, and its recency\n  position unchanged.\n- **FR-016**: Every stored value MUST cost exactly one capacity slot\n  regardless of object size or shape.\n\n### Key Entities *(include if feature involves data)*\n\n- **Cache entry**: A live mapping from one hashable key to one stored value\n  plus the set of tags it carries; occupies exactly one of the maxsize slots.\n- **Tag**: A string label shared by any number of live entries; used only for\n  bulk invalidation. A tag's membership set always reflects live entries only.\n- **Recency order**: The LRU ordering over live entries, updated on put (new\n  or replaced) and on get hits; determines which entry is evicted when the\n  cache is full.\n\n## Success Criteria *(mandatory)*\n\n### Measurable Outcomes\n\n- **SC-001**: 100% of documented behaviors (FR-001 through FR-016) are\n  verified by passing tests, including the public acceptance suite\n  (acceptance_public.py) and project-owned tests; zero failures at\n  convergence.\n- **SC-002**: 100% of the existing public cachetools API keeps its existing\n  behavior: existing upstream tests pass unchanged, and no existing name,\n  signature, or documented behavior is altered or removed.\n- **SC-003**: In every invalid-input scenario (bad maxsize, bare-string tags,\n  non-string tag entries, uncopyable value), state preservation is 100%\n  observable: prior value, tag memberships, and recency order are exactly as\n  before the failed call.\n- **SC-004**: Tag accounting is exact in all tested sequences: invalidate\n  returns the precise live-entry count, len equals the live-entry count, and\n  no evicted or invalidated entry is ever counted, removed, or refreshed.\n- **SC-005**: Retrieved values are fully detached in every test: mutations to\n  returned objects (including nested members) and to caller originals after\n  put have zero observable effect on stored content.\n\n## Assumptions\n\n- Target users are Python library consumers of cachetools; the feature is a\n  new additive module (cachetools.tagged) and no user-facing configuration or\n  documentation site changes are in scope.\n- \"Deep copy\" follows the standard Python deep-copy semantics for supported\n  value types; values that cannot be deep-copied raise the copy mechanism's\n  error, and the failed put leaves prior state unchanged (FR-015).\n- Validation order: maxsize is checked at construction; tags are validated\n  before value copying, so a tag error never depends on value copyability.\n- Unhashable keys raise TypeError from standard Python hashing on put; this\n  is the language contract, not a feature-specific error.\n- Recency semantics: putting a new key makes it most-recently-used; getting a\n  hit makes that entry most-recently-used. (Whether replacing an existing key\n  via put also refreshes its recency is a candidate extension, not part of\n  this baseline; see Out of Scope.)\n- The public acceptance suite (acceptance_public.py) and upstream tests are\n  the authoritative verification; project-owned tests may be added but\n  upstream tests must remain unchanged.\n\n## Out of Scope\n\n- The Claim-A candidate extension (variant put_no_recency_refresh,\n  2026-09-21): \"overwriting an existing key via put refreshes its LRU\n  recency, making it the most-recently-used entry.\" It is recorded here as a\n  deferred intent for a later phase; the baseline spec (R01-R03) does not\n  require it and does not forbid it. If a later phase adopts it, the spec and\n  this section MUST be updated in the same change.\n- Thread safety, persistence, serialization, and any non-LRU eviction\n  policies.\n- Changes to existing cachetools classes or modules.\n"}